WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.380
Hello, my name is Glenn Loury.

2
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:18.330
I'm the Merton Stoltz professor of the Social Sciences

3
00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:21.870
at Brown University and professor of Economics there,

4
00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:23.360
and I've been writing for many years

5
00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:26.090
about the issues of race and inequality

6
00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:28.160
in the United States.

7
00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:31.210
I'm here to talk about race in America,

8
00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:34.040
with particular emphasis on the black family.

9
00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.470
I just wanna share with you some thoughts that I prepared

10
00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:37.633
for this occasion.

11
00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:43.210
I wanna talk about the fact of historical

12
00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:45.700
African-American subordination.

13
00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:49.920
I wanna say what it means for American citizenship,

14
00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:53.760
and I wanna consider some possible solutions

15
00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.453
to this longstanding dilemma.

16
00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:58.880
The priority of social relations

17
00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:02.203
before economic transactions is my theme.

18
00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:06.040
But don't worry, I'll not be masquerading as a sociologist

19
00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:07.190
or a political theorist.

20
00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:09.560
Rather, I'm gonna draw on my many years of study

21
00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:13.080
as an economist and a public intellectual

22
00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.670
to briefly make two observations

23
00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:17.520
and then draw some conclusions.

24
00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.030
One about the dynamics of human development

25
00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:23.420
and the other about the foundations of racial identity.

26
00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:25.970
I will bring this back to the theme of the black family

27
00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:27.797
in conclusion.

28
00:01:27.797 --> 00:01:29.960
"Now, why?" I ask.

29
00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:33.200
The success of the Civil Rights Movement not withstanding

30
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.560
has the subordinate status of African-Americans persisted

31
00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:39.690
in the 21st century.

32
00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:41.960
Clear thinking about this intractable problem

33
00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.380
requires one to distinguish the role played

34
00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:47.130
by discrimination against black people

35
00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:50.040
from the role of counterproductive patterns of behavior

36
00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:54.330
that can be found within African-American society.

37
00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:58.540
Now, I admit that this puts what is a very sensitive issue,

38
00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:00.370
rather starkly.

39
00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:05.200
Many vocal advocates of racial equality

40
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.370
refuse even to consider the possibility

41
00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:10.840
that problematic behavior could be an important factor

42
00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:15.210
contributing to the persisting disadvantage of blacks.

43
00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:16.043
At the same time,

44
00:02:16.043 --> 00:02:19.000
some observers on the right of American politics

45
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.190
insists that anti-black discrimination

46
00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:23.020
is no longer an important determinant

47
00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:25.260
of unequal social outcomes.

48
00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:28.160
I've tried to chart a middle course

49
00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:31.680
acknowledging anti-black biases that should be remedied,

50
00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.080
but insisting on the imperative of addressing

51
00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.330
and reversing the behavior patterns

52
00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:37.920
preventing some blacks from seizing

53
00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.500
newly opened opportunities.

54
00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.120
Among these behavior patterns are gonna be the interactions

55
00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:45.270
between men and women within the context

56
00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:49.570
of family formation, childbearing, and child rearing.

57
00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:53.430
This is an explosive topic in American political culture.

58
00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:55.890
I can remember within my own lifetime

59
00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:58.801
when Daniel Patrick Moynihan's report was first issued

60
00:02:58.801 --> 00:03:03.801
in 1965, "The Negro Family: The Case for National Action"

61
00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:05.310
he called it.

62
00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:08.730
He argued then with 25% of black children

63
00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:10.710
being born to a woman without a husband

64
00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:14.000
that this problem was gonna stand as a fundamental barrier

65
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.420
to black progress.

66
00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:17.370
And indeed, if it weren't somehow dealt with

67
00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:20.390
might prevent the fruit of the Civil Rights Movement

68
00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:24.950
successes from being enjoyed fully by African-Americans

69
00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:26.520
and by the country.

70
00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:30.500
He was pilloried for daring to raise the question

71
00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:32.010
as a white man.

72
00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:33.510
And since then,

73
00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.710
a political discussion about this question

74
00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:38.810
has been stifled and truncated.

75
00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:42.270
People have been wary to raise the issues

76
00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:44.570
and to talk about these themes.

77
00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:48.000
They don't wanna be accused of blaming the victim.

78
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.260
They don't wanna seem to be judgmental.

79
00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:53.560
They don't wanna be imposing a moral calculus

80
00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:56.750
on the lives of people,

81
00:03:56.750 --> 00:04:00.180
especially people who have been victimized by discrimination

82
00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:02.750
and who may be plagued by poverty.

83
00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:04.870
This, I think, is a mistake.

84
00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:09.870
What was 25% when Moynihan was calling his call to arms

85
00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:14.210
was issuing his call to arms over a half century ago.

86
00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:16.320
It's 70% now.

87
00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.410
The African-American family, one could say,

88
00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:20.210
is in deep trouble.

89
00:04:20.210 --> 00:04:22.020
Again, there will be pushback.

90
00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:25.270
People will say, "Why do you judge family by a single norm?"

91
00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:27.400
They will say, "It takes a village to raise a child.

92
00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:29.860
Why should we think in terms of patrimony,"

93
00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:31.430
patriarchy, I should say,

94
00:04:31.430 --> 00:04:34.570
in terms of the nuclear family is the only model.

95
00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:37.330
I don't wanna waste my time with those arguments.

96
00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:40.480
I simply wanna call attention to the needs of young people

97
00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:43.130
that have stable social foundations

98
00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:45.950
within the context of which they can mature

99
00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:48.450
and realize their human potential.

100
00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:51.000
And I wanna say that a mother and father raising children

101
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:54.860
is a sounder foundation to that end

102
00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:57.290
than as a mother raising those children alone.

103
00:04:57.290 --> 00:05:00.030
I'll have more to say about that in due course.

104
00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:02.190
But let me return to the framework

105
00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:03.960
that I wanna try to elaborate for you

106
00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:07.460
by discussing those two observations.

107
00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:11.880
These two positions can be recast as causal narratives.

108
00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.040
One I'm gonna call the biased narrative.

109
00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:16.760
Racism and white supremacy have done this wrong.

110
00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:19.100
We can't get ahead until they relent,

111
00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:22.620
so we must continue urging reform on American society

112
00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:23.453
toward that end.

113
00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:25.660
The other narrative I'm gonna call

114
00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:27.450
the development narrative.

115
00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:31.130
Emphasizing the need to consider how people come to acquire

116
00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:35.000
those skills, traits, habits, and orientations

117
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:38.300
that foster an individual's successful participation

118
00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:40.380
in American society.

119
00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:41.910
To the extent that black youngsters

120
00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:44.030
do not have the experiences,

121
00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:46.060
are not exposed to the influences,

122
00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:48.600
and do not benefit from the resources

123
00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:51.060
that foster and facilitate their human development

124
00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:52.400
to that extent,

125
00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:55.520
they may fail to achieve their full potential.

126
00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:57.210
It is this lack of development

127
00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:00.050
that ultimately causes the stark racial disparities

128
00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:02.820
in income, wealth, education,

129
00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.680
and much else to persist,

130
00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:06.790
so goes the development narrative.

131
00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:08.380
So we have two narratives,

132
00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:11.550
the bias narrative and the development narrative.

133
00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:13.870
We have two basic approaches to the problem

134
00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:16.100
of persisting racial inequality.

135
00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:19.170
To assume that it's the consequences of discrimination

136
00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:21.510
or to understand that, at least in part,

137
00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:24.580
is the result of the failure of many African-Americans

138
00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:27.130
to realize their full human potential.

139
00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:29.600
I wanna emphasize this latter narrative

140
00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:32.170
as being more relevant to our present day.

141
00:06:32.170 --> 00:06:35.400
And I wanna call attention to the role of the family

142
00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:38.280
as a part of the larger developmental matrix

143
00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:41.290
which either fosters or impedes the potential

144
00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:45.073
for achievement for black people in America.

145
00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:48.920
These two narratives, of course,

146
00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:51.143
need not be mutually exclusive.

147
00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:54.710
But in terms of prescribing interventions and remedies,

148
00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:56.410
they point in very different directions.

149
00:06:56.410 --> 00:06:58.930
The bias narrative urges us to,

150
00:06:58.930 --> 00:07:01.740
well, to have a conversation about race.

151
00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:03.870
White America must reform itself.

152
00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:05.810
Racism must end.

153
00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:07.730
We need more of this or more of that,

154
00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:10.540
whatever the this or the that is on the agenda

155
00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:13.290
of today's social justice warriors.

156
00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:16.360
One can hear this kind of talk and read these exhortations

157
00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:19.543
in newspapers and other media daily.

158
00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:22.170
The development narrative,

159
00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:25.610
alternatively, puts more onus on the responsibilities

160
00:07:25.610 --> 00:07:29.460
of African-Americans to develop our human potential.

161
00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:31.480
Not on our own to be sure,

162
00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:33.510
not without the support and the assistance

163
00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:37.020
of whatever social programs the society should decide

164
00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:38.290
are justified.

165
00:07:38.290 --> 00:07:40.320
But nevertheless, ultimately,

166
00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:44.130
the responsibility for raising one's children into maturity

167
00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:48.800
rests on the parents of those children first and foremost.

168
00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:50.940
The development narrative is not satisfied

169
00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:52.380
with wishful thinking,

170
00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:55.420
like if we could only double the budget for some social

171
00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:57.910
program than the homicide rate among young black men

172
00:07:57.910 --> 00:07:59.070
would be less atrocious.

173
00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:02.620
Or if we could just get the local police department

174
00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:04.700
investigated by the U.S Department of Justice,

175
00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:05.533
then...

176
00:08:06.567 --> 00:08:10.670
"Then what?" the development narrative wants to ask.

177
00:08:10.670 --> 00:08:12.940
Then it will become possible to walk on the south side

178
00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:14.563
of Chicago after midnight?

179
00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:17.610
I am one social scientist who finds that

180
00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:20.133
to be an extremely dubious claim.

181
00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:23.763
So, what are my two observations?

182
00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:28.340
Over four decades ago in my doctoral dissertation at MIT,

183
00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:32.273
I had the good fortune to coin the term social capital.

184
00:08:33.430 --> 00:08:36.240
I did so by way of contrasting my concept,

185
00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:39.480
social capital, with what economists then

186
00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:41.873
traditionally called human capital.

187
00:08:42.730 --> 00:08:43.563
As you may know,

188
00:08:43.563 --> 00:08:46.190
human capital theory imports into the study

189
00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:49.260
of human inequality and intellectual framework

190
00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:51.470
which had been developed primarily

191
00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:54.270
to explain investment decisions by firms,

192
00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:56.230
a framework that focuses on the analysis

193
00:08:56.230 --> 00:08:59.360
of formal economic transactions.

194
00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:02.790
In my thesis, I argued that this framework was inadequate

195
00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:06.290
to the problem of accounting for social inequality.

196
00:09:06.290 --> 00:09:08.103
Please, allow me to explain.

197
00:09:09.770 --> 00:09:12.770
My fundamental point was that associating business

198
00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:17.290
and human investments is merely an analogy, not an identity,

199
00:09:17.290 --> 00:09:19.490
particularly if one seeks to explain

200
00:09:19.490 --> 00:09:22.100
persistent racial disparities.

201
00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:24.240
Business investments are transactional.

202
00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:26.850
Human investments are essentially relational.

203
00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:28.520
So important things are overlooked

204
00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:30.280
in the human capital approach,

205
00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:33.720
things having to do with informal social relations,

206
00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:37.640
such as those which occur inside of families.

207
00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:39.740
Human capital theory is incomplete

208
00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:43.610
when it comes to explaining racial disparities, I argued.

209
00:09:43.610 --> 00:09:46.400
There are two central aspects of this incompleteness

210
00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:48.970
and thus my two observations about the dynamics

211
00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:53.170
of human development and the nature of racial identity.

212
00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:54.313
Permit me to explain.

213
00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:58.160
First, I stress that all human development

214
00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:01.550
is socially situated and mediated.

215
00:10:01.550 --> 00:10:04.300
That is, I argued that the development of human beings

216
00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:07.170
occurs inside of social institutions.

217
00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:09.460
It takes place as between people

218
00:10:09.460 --> 00:10:11.120
by way of human interaction,

219
00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:15.490
the family, the community, the school, the peer group,

220
00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:18.530
these cultural institutions of human association

221
00:10:18.530 --> 00:10:21.330
are where development is achieved,

222
00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:24.033
resources essential to human development.

223
00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:28.360
The attention that a parent gives to her child,

224
00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:31.087
for instance, are not alienable.

225
00:10:32.140 --> 00:10:34.310
Developmental resources, for the most part,

226
00:10:34.310 --> 00:10:36.250
are not commodities.

227
00:10:36.250 --> 00:10:40.900
The development of human beings is largely not up for sale.

228
00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:44.140
Rather, structured connections between individuals

229
00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:47.670
create the context within which developmental resources

230
00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:50.700
come to be allocated to individual persons.

231
00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:52.340
I ask you to forgive me.

232
00:10:52.340 --> 00:10:53.760
I speak like an economist.

233
00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:56.980
I talk about resources and I talk about allocations.

234
00:10:56.980 --> 00:10:58.010
I can't help myself.

235
00:10:58.010 --> 00:10:59.540
I am an economist.

236
00:10:59.540 --> 00:11:02.010
But bear with me because these categories are,

237
00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:04.890
I think, useful as a conceptual framework

238
00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:07.560
for talking about the problem of inequality,

239
00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:10.653
which is what I am intending to talk about here today.

240
00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:16.003
Opportunity travels along the synapsis of social networks.

241
00:11:17.180 --> 00:11:19.400
The development of human beings is not the same

242
00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:20.470
as corporate investment,

243
00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:23.450
and so, it need not be a good metaphor or a good analogy

244
00:11:23.450 --> 00:11:25.940
to reason as though this were so.

245
00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:28.080
People are not machines.

246
00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:30.500
Their productivities, that is to say,

247
00:11:30.500 --> 00:11:33.670
the behavioral and cognitive capacities

248
00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:36.480
bearing on their social and economic function,

249
00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:38.360
these things are not merely the result

250
00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:42.590
of a mechanical infusion of material resources.

251
00:11:42.590 --> 00:11:46.150
Redistributing money doesn't necessarily fix the problem

252
00:11:46.150 --> 00:11:49.460
when these things are in deficit.

253
00:11:49.460 --> 00:11:52.960
Rather, the capacities to develop human beings

254
00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:55.390
are the byproducts of social processes

255
00:11:55.390 --> 00:11:58.240
that are mediated by networks of human affiliation

256
00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:00.210
and connectivity.

257
00:12:00.210 --> 00:12:02.420
This was fundamentally important, I thought,

258
00:12:02.420 --> 00:12:03.820
and still think,

259
00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:06.730
for understanding persistent racial disparities in America.

260
00:12:06.730 --> 00:12:09.280
And that's why I coined the term social capital

261
00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:12.610
in order to emphasize the difference between the benefits

262
00:12:12.610 --> 00:12:16.420
to human development that flow from social connectivity

263
00:12:16.420 --> 00:12:19.593
and those benefits that flow from monetary resources.

264
00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:23.350
That's the first point that I wanted to make

265
00:12:23.350 --> 00:12:25.010
all those years ago

266
00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:28.130
about the incompleteness of human capital theory.

267
00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:33.130
Now, my second observation was that what we are calling race

268
00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:35.840
in America is mainly a social

269
00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:39.170
and only indirectly a biological phenomenon.

270
00:12:39.170 --> 00:12:42.730
Mind you now, I'm talking about racial inequality in America

271
00:12:42.730 --> 00:12:44.120
and the black family.

272
00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:48.200
So I'm talking about inequality and I'm talking about race.

273
00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:51.270
My first observation was an observation about inequality.

274
00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:53.390
Inequality in human development

275
00:12:53.390 --> 00:12:57.330
because of the way that social networks either do or do not

276
00:12:57.330 --> 00:13:00.350
deliver the relations that are necessary

277
00:13:00.350 --> 00:13:03.950
in order for people to flourish and reach their potential.

278
00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:05.520
Now I wanna talk about race.

279
00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:07.770
And in America, that is not only,

280
00:13:07.770 --> 00:13:09.610
but it is substantially in our history,

281
00:13:09.610 --> 00:13:12.070
a matter of black and white.

282
00:13:12.070 --> 00:13:13.670
And so, as I go forward,

283
00:13:13.670 --> 00:13:16.860
I will talk about race in somewhat binary terms,

284
00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:20.840
which is not entirely descriptive of the complex demographic

285
00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:23.520
landscape that we inhabit here in the 21st century

286
00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:25.330
in the United States of America,

287
00:13:25.330 --> 00:13:27.890
but what should be a useful starting point

288
00:13:27.890 --> 00:13:30.180
for thinking about the problem of racial inequality

289
00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:32.790
and persisting black disadvantage,

290
00:13:32.790 --> 00:13:35.010
which is the subject of this lecture.

291
00:13:35.010 --> 00:13:38.340
So, my second observation was that what we're calling race

292
00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:39.960
in America is mainly a social

293
00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:42.473
and only indirectly a biological phenomenon.

294
00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:47.050
The persistence across generations of racial differentiation

295
00:13:47.050 --> 00:13:50.940
between large groups of people in an open society

296
00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:54.370
where individuals live in close proximity to one another

297
00:13:55.400 --> 00:13:58.940
provides irrefutable indirect evidence of a profound

298
00:13:58.940 --> 00:14:02.930
separation between the racially defined social networks

299
00:14:02.930 --> 00:14:05.940
of affiliation within that society.

300
00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:08.050
That was a somewhat complicated sentence,

301
00:14:08.050 --> 00:14:10.260
so let me try to put it more directly.

302
00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:14.780
There would be no races in the study state of any dynamic

303
00:14:14.780 --> 00:14:18.140
social system unless on a daily basis,

304
00:14:18.140 --> 00:14:20.990
and in regard to their most intimate affairs,

305
00:14:20.990 --> 00:14:24.410
people paid very close attention to the boundaries

306
00:14:24.410 --> 00:14:28.383
that separate themselves from racially distinct others.

307
00:14:30.580 --> 00:14:32.820
Over time, that is,

308
00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:35.160
race, in inverted commas,

309
00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:39.360
would cease to exist unless people chose to act in a manner

310
00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:43.270
so as biologically to reproduce the variety

311
00:14:43.270 --> 00:14:46.690
of phenotypic expression that constitutes the subject

312
00:14:46.690 --> 00:14:49.330
of racial distinction.

313
00:14:49.330 --> 00:14:52.470
Again, a complex sentence packed with ideas

314
00:14:52.470 --> 00:14:54.520
that are important to the subject of our discussion

315
00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:55.690
here right now.

316
00:14:55.690 --> 00:14:58.030
I can't overemphasize the point I'm trying to make

317
00:14:58.030 --> 00:14:58.950
at this moment.

318
00:14:58.950 --> 00:15:01.750
What we're calling race is not something that is given

319
00:15:01.750 --> 00:15:02.683
in nature.

320
00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:06.640
Rather it is a social product.

321
00:15:06.640 --> 00:15:08.720
It's an equilibrium outcome.

322
00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:10.520
It's something that we, collectively,

323
00:15:10.520 --> 00:15:14.490
within the society are making and remaking over time.

324
00:15:14.490 --> 00:15:17.020
It is, as we economists like to say,

325
00:15:17.020 --> 00:15:19.113
an indogenous outcome.

326
00:15:20.500 --> 00:15:23.170
It follows that if the goal is to understand the roots

327
00:15:23.170 --> 00:15:25.260
of durable racial inequality,

328
00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:27.470
one needs to attend, in some detail,

329
00:15:27.470 --> 00:15:31.340
to the processes that cause race to persist as a fact

330
00:15:31.340 --> 00:15:32.640
in the society under study

331
00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:35.490
because those processes, almost certainly,

332
00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:37.630
will not be unrelated to the allocation

333
00:15:37.630 --> 00:15:41.060
of developmental resources in that society.

334
00:15:41.060 --> 00:15:43.590
Again, I need to apologize for being an economist.

335
00:15:43.590 --> 00:15:46.610
I'm talking about the allocation of developmental resources.

336
00:15:46.610 --> 00:15:49.460
My first point was that that depends on how people

337
00:15:49.460 --> 00:15:53.240
are located relative to each other within social networks.

338
00:15:53.240 --> 00:15:55.000
It depends upon relations.

339
00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:57.640
But I'm also talking about racial inequality.

340
00:15:57.640 --> 00:16:00.830
And my second observation is that depends on how people

341
00:16:00.830 --> 00:16:03.660
in that society behave in terms of their most intimate

342
00:16:03.660 --> 00:16:06.220
social relations with one another

343
00:16:06.220 --> 00:16:08.750
because without mating patterns that are largely

344
00:16:08.750 --> 00:16:10.470
within racial groups,

345
00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:14.340
the very fact of distinct races within the society

346
00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:17.400
would fade away and wither away over time.

347
00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:20.090
So those are the two observations that I wanna make

348
00:16:20.090 --> 00:16:23.130
as a foundation for carrying forward my argument

349
00:16:23.130 --> 00:16:24.563
about the black family.

350
00:16:26.030 --> 00:16:29.630
Here then is my second observation in a nutshell.

351
00:16:29.630 --> 00:16:33.430
The creation and reproduction of race as a social reality

352
00:16:33.430 --> 00:16:38.430
in any society rests on cultural conceptions about identity

353
00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:40.000
that are embraced by people,

354
00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:43.620
blacks and whites alike, in that society.

355
00:16:43.620 --> 00:16:46.540
These are the convictions people affirm

356
00:16:46.540 --> 00:16:49.070
about who they are and about the legitimacy

357
00:16:49.070 --> 00:16:51.450
of conducting intimate relations

358
00:16:51.450 --> 00:16:53.050
with racially distinct others.

359
00:16:53.050 --> 00:16:56.143
And here I do not only mean sexual relations.

360
00:16:57.120 --> 00:16:59.960
My impulse to contrast human and social capital

361
00:16:59.960 --> 00:17:02.590
all those years ago was rooted in my conviction

362
00:17:02.590 --> 00:17:04.670
that beliefs of this kind

363
00:17:04.670 --> 00:17:07.150
ultimately determine the access that people enjoy

364
00:17:07.150 --> 00:17:09.790
to the informal resources required to develop

365
00:17:09.790 --> 00:17:11.760
their human potential.

366
00:17:11.760 --> 00:17:16.600
What I called social capital in 1976 was on this view,

367
00:17:16.600 --> 00:17:20.270
a critical prerequisite for creating what economists

368
00:17:20.270 --> 00:17:23.140
typically referred to as human capital.

369
00:17:23.140 --> 00:17:25.170
This point again is crucial

370
00:17:26.020 --> 00:17:27.810
if we're to understand the persistence

371
00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:29.663
of racial inequality in America.

372
00:17:31.040 --> 00:17:32.767
But what you're gonna ask yourselves,

373
00:17:32.767 --> 00:17:33.960
"Is this to do with the black family?"

374
00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:35.020
Well, perhaps.

375
00:17:35.020 --> 00:17:37.840
Perhaps that connection is becoming clear

376
00:17:37.840 --> 00:17:40.280
because the black family is the context

377
00:17:40.280 --> 00:17:45.280
within which African-Americans are situated,

378
00:17:46.720 --> 00:17:49.790
especially in the earliest years of life

379
00:17:49.790 --> 00:17:52.190
and where these dynamics of human development

380
00:17:52.190 --> 00:17:55.373
are playing out for better or for ill.

381
00:17:56.580 --> 00:17:58.870
Historically oppressed groups time and again

382
00:17:58.870 --> 00:18:00.330
have evolved notions of identity

383
00:18:00.330 --> 00:18:04.220
that cut against the grain of their society's mainstream.

384
00:18:04.220 --> 00:18:08.030
A culture can develop among them that inhibits talented

385
00:18:08.030 --> 00:18:10.420
youngsters from taking the actions needed

386
00:18:10.420 --> 00:18:12.830
to develop their talent.

387
00:18:12.830 --> 00:18:14.780
Now, given such a situation,

388
00:18:14.780 --> 00:18:16.580
I wanna ask,

389
00:18:16.580 --> 00:18:19.270
do the kids in dysfunctional peer groups,

390
00:18:19.270 --> 00:18:22.140
or from families that are poorly structured,

391
00:18:22.140 --> 00:18:24.080
simply have the wrong utility functions

392
00:18:24.080 --> 00:18:27.280
or do those communities simply have the wrong values?

393
00:18:27.280 --> 00:18:28.810
Now, I wanna hold that it's a mistake

394
00:18:28.810 --> 00:18:30.720
to attribute the dysfunctional behavior

395
00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:33.100
of a historically oppressed group

396
00:18:33.100 --> 00:18:35.120
to they're simply having the wrong preferences

397
00:18:35.120 --> 00:18:38.190
when those preferences have emerged from a set of historical

398
00:18:38.190 --> 00:18:41.640
experiences that reflect the largest society structures

399
00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:43.020
and activities.

400
00:18:43.020 --> 00:18:45.510
It's a mistake to simply blame the victim.

401
00:18:45.510 --> 00:18:46.343
On the other hand,

402
00:18:46.343 --> 00:18:49.300
it's also a mistake to ignore patterns of behavior

403
00:18:49.300 --> 00:18:50.920
within population groups

404
00:18:50.920 --> 00:18:54.500
that may inhibit their ability to take full advantage

405
00:18:54.500 --> 00:18:57.880
of the opportunities that are present in their society.

406
00:18:57.880 --> 00:19:00.180
You can make a mistake in blaming the victim

407
00:19:00.180 --> 00:19:03.380
and you can make a mistake in looking away from the role

408
00:19:03.380 --> 00:19:06.170
that the victim quote unquote might play

409
00:19:06.170 --> 00:19:09.710
in his or her own bad situation.

410
00:19:09.710 --> 00:19:12.970
Another way of saying this is that social relations

411
00:19:12.970 --> 00:19:15.543
come before economic transactions.

412
00:19:16.780 --> 00:19:19.060
When ethnic communities and their local cultures

413
00:19:19.060 --> 00:19:22.630
are not integrated across boundaries of race in a society,

414
00:19:22.630 --> 00:19:24.560
then racial inequality can persist,

415
00:19:24.560 --> 00:19:27.770
not withstanding the enactment and effective enforcement

416
00:19:27.770 --> 00:19:29.563
of laws against discrimination.

417
00:19:31.460 --> 00:19:33.230
The persistence of racial disparities

418
00:19:33.230 --> 00:19:35.400
derives not simply from discrimination,

419
00:19:35.400 --> 00:19:38.060
but more fundamentally from the complex

420
00:19:38.060 --> 00:19:41.660
morally ambiguous and difficult to regulate phenomena

421
00:19:41.660 --> 00:19:45.750
that embody and reflect what people see as the meanings

422
00:19:45.750 --> 00:19:48.140
given significance to their lives

423
00:19:48.140 --> 00:19:51.500
and from the structure of the social connectivity

424
00:19:51.500 --> 00:19:54.310
to which those meanings give rise.

425
00:19:54.310 --> 00:19:58.890
All of this leads me to an important conclusion.

426
00:19:58.890 --> 00:20:02.597
Namely, how a diverse society answers the question,

427
00:20:02.597 --> 00:20:04.510
"Who are we?"

428
00:20:04.510 --> 00:20:06.693
Is a fundamentally significant issue.

429
00:20:07.600 --> 00:20:08.433
Who are we?

430
00:20:09.690 --> 00:20:10.863
Whose country is it?

431
00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:15.010
When we talk about crime, violence, school failure,

432
00:20:15.010 --> 00:20:18.650
urban decay, or family life for that matter,

433
00:20:18.650 --> 00:20:20.410
are these matters in the back of our mind

434
00:20:20.410 --> 00:20:23.720
that we understand as us and them?

435
00:20:23.720 --> 00:20:25.560
Because if it's us against them,

436
00:20:25.560 --> 00:20:26.790
anything is possible.

437
00:20:26.790 --> 00:20:28.200
It becomes possible, for example,

438
00:20:28.200 --> 00:20:31.160
to say about those people languishing in the ghettos

439
00:20:31.160 --> 00:20:32.910
of our great cities.

440
00:20:32.910 --> 00:20:34.340
That's not my country.

441
00:20:34.340 --> 00:20:35.173
That's not me.

442
00:20:35.173 --> 00:20:36.310
Those aren't my people.

443
00:20:36.310 --> 00:20:39.233
That's some third world thing or whatever.

444
00:20:40.270 --> 00:20:42.690
I mention this because we're talking about family life.

445
00:20:42.690 --> 00:20:44.930
We're talking about intimate personal relations.

446
00:20:44.930 --> 00:20:47.190
I'm arguing that inequality, to some degree,

447
00:20:47.190 --> 00:20:49.440
is the consequence of a failure

448
00:20:49.440 --> 00:20:51.180
or a dysfunction in the family

449
00:20:51.180 --> 00:20:53.460
lives of black people.

450
00:20:53.460 --> 00:20:55.050
I'm a black person myself.

451
00:20:55.050 --> 00:20:58.220
I don't take any pleasure in making this argument.

452
00:20:58.220 --> 00:21:00.160
I feel compelled to make it.

453
00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:00.993
But in doing so,

454
00:21:00.993 --> 00:21:03.340
I don't wanna join a chorus of critics

455
00:21:03.340 --> 00:21:05.800
who stand on the outside and point fingers

456
00:21:05.800 --> 00:21:09.060
at those people and talk about their deficiencies.

457
00:21:09.060 --> 00:21:11.010
I don't want to be a hypocritical moralist

458
00:21:11.010 --> 00:21:13.460
who wags my finger in their faces

459
00:21:13.460 --> 00:21:15.550
and tell them that they should live quote,

460
00:21:15.550 --> 00:21:17.310
better close quote.

461
00:21:17.310 --> 00:21:19.240
But I do wanna be a realist.

462
00:21:19.240 --> 00:21:21.380
I don't wanna look away from the consequences

463
00:21:21.380 --> 00:21:23.460
of the way in which many of them are,

464
00:21:23.460 --> 00:21:25.730
as a matter of fact, living.

465
00:21:25.730 --> 00:21:27.100
In doing so, however,

466
00:21:27.100 --> 00:21:28.700
I don't wanna give license to those

467
00:21:28.700 --> 00:21:31.100
who wanna wipe their hands of the matter,

468
00:21:31.100 --> 00:21:34.780
turn their backs on these people who need our help,

469
00:21:34.780 --> 00:21:37.080
and simply go on about their business.

470
00:21:37.080 --> 00:21:39.500
And so, that's why I ask the question,

471
00:21:39.500 --> 00:21:42.110
who are we here in America?

472
00:21:42.110 --> 00:21:43.810
My point is that these problems,

473
00:21:43.810 --> 00:21:46.330
including the problems of African-American family life,

474
00:21:46.330 --> 00:21:50.770
are quintessentially an American affair

475
00:21:50.770 --> 00:21:55.240
not simply a measure of the inadequacy

476
00:21:55.240 --> 00:21:57.360
of black culture.

477
00:21:57.360 --> 00:22:01.190
They reflect upon our social inadequacy,

478
00:22:01.190 --> 00:22:02.160
I wish to argue,

479
00:22:02.160 --> 00:22:04.923
and by our, I mean, all of us.

480
00:22:06.040 --> 00:22:06.923
For example.

481
00:22:08.170 --> 00:22:12.100
Much of the decline in the structure of African-American

482
00:22:12.100 --> 00:22:14.170
family, I say decline because I mean the rise

483
00:22:14.170 --> 00:22:17.150
of out of wedlock birth and the decline in the prevalence

484
00:22:17.150 --> 00:22:19.670
of husband, wife households,

485
00:22:19.670 --> 00:22:20.610
much of that decline,

486
00:22:20.610 --> 00:22:23.910
I think, can be laid at the feet of the consequences

487
00:22:23.910 --> 00:22:25.420
of American welfare policy

488
00:22:25.420 --> 00:22:29.790
which has created incentives for family dissolution.

489
00:22:29.790 --> 00:22:30.760
Much of it, I think,

490
00:22:30.760 --> 00:22:33.750
could be laid at the feet of drifts in American culture

491
00:22:33.750 --> 00:22:38.750
in which the virtues of a monogamous and a husband, wife

492
00:22:39.020 --> 00:22:41.420
families has declined amongst all groups

493
00:22:41.420 --> 00:22:44.170
reflected in the popular media and so forth.

494
00:22:44.170 --> 00:22:45.347
Black people in the United States

495
00:22:45.347 --> 00:22:46.980
are not on an island.

496
00:22:46.980 --> 00:22:50.340
They live integrated within the larger social fabric.

497
00:22:50.340 --> 00:22:52.440
And so, patterns of behavior that we see among them

498
00:22:52.440 --> 00:22:54.550
are not simply on them,

499
00:22:54.550 --> 00:22:58.860
they are also, to some degree, on us.

500
00:22:58.860 --> 00:23:01.440
This is what I mean when being an economist,

501
00:23:01.440 --> 00:23:04.130
I nevertheless insist on placing relations

502
00:23:04.130 --> 00:23:06.740
before transactions.

503
00:23:06.740 --> 00:23:08.210
What I'm talking about now,

504
00:23:08.210 --> 00:23:12.120
in other words, is the American story,

505
00:23:12.120 --> 00:23:14.800
not just the black American story.

506
00:23:14.800 --> 00:23:16.670
Let me conclude.

507
00:23:16.670 --> 00:23:18.730
Consider the poor central city dwellers

508
00:23:18.730 --> 00:23:20.180
who now make up maybe a quarter

509
00:23:20.180 --> 00:23:22.640
of the black American population.

510
00:23:22.640 --> 00:23:26.350
The dysfunctional behavior of many in this population

511
00:23:26.350 --> 00:23:28.530
is a big part of the problem here.

512
00:23:28.530 --> 00:23:32.610
So, conservatives demand for greater personal responsibility

513
00:23:32.610 --> 00:23:36.250
in these quarters is necessary and proper.

514
00:23:36.250 --> 00:23:39.280
And yet, confronted with the despair, violence,

515
00:23:39.280 --> 00:23:42.314
and self-destructive folly of so many people,

516
00:23:42.314 --> 00:23:46.770
it is morally and intellectually superficial

517
00:23:46.770 --> 00:23:50.110
in the extreme to argue as many have done

518
00:23:50.110 --> 00:23:54.150
that those people should just get their acts together

519
00:23:54.150 --> 00:23:55.860
like many of the poor immigrants.

520
00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:56.950
If they did,

521
00:23:56.950 --> 00:24:00.210
we wouldn't have such a horrific problem in our cities.

522
00:24:00.210 --> 00:24:02.300
To the contrary, I would say,

523
00:24:02.300 --> 00:24:06.160
any morally astute response to the social pathology

524
00:24:06.160 --> 00:24:08.720
of American history's losers

525
00:24:08.720 --> 00:24:11.080
should conclude that while we cannot change

526
00:24:11.080 --> 00:24:12.520
our ignoble past,

527
00:24:12.520 --> 00:24:15.500
we need not and must not be indifferent to the contemporary

528
00:24:15.500 --> 00:24:16.793
consequences of it.

529
00:24:18.870 --> 00:24:22.563
Their culture may be implicated in their difficulties.

530
00:24:24.490 --> 00:24:29.490
But then so too, is our culture complicit in their troubles.

531
00:24:29.970 --> 00:24:31.650
We, and I mean all Americans,

532
00:24:31.650 --> 00:24:35.230
bear collective responsibility for the form and the texture

533
00:24:35.230 --> 00:24:37.970
of our society's social relations,

534
00:24:37.970 --> 00:24:40.490
and racial discrimination and racial segregation

535
00:24:40.490 --> 00:24:42.980
is a part of that structure and texture.

536
00:24:42.980 --> 00:24:47.010
It is also a part of the foundation for a racial inequality

537
00:24:47.010 --> 00:24:48.010
in the country.

538
00:24:48.010 --> 00:24:50.610
I am not talking here out of both sides of my mouth.

539
00:24:51.530 --> 00:24:54.870
I insist on recognizing the importance of the family,

540
00:24:54.870 --> 00:24:57.670
acknowledging the decline of structure of family

541
00:24:57.670 --> 00:24:59.180
amongst African-Americans,

542
00:24:59.180 --> 00:25:02.090
and owning the consequences of that decline

543
00:25:02.090 --> 00:25:05.350
in terms of its contribution to racial inequality.

544
00:25:05.350 --> 00:25:08.380
But I'm also not a moral infant.

545
00:25:08.380 --> 00:25:11.440
I know that having recognized those social connections

546
00:25:11.440 --> 00:25:14.250
and the role that they play in persisting inequality,

547
00:25:14.250 --> 00:25:16.900
I have not solved the problem of what is the right thing

548
00:25:16.900 --> 00:25:19.210
to do for the society as a whole.

549
00:25:19.210 --> 00:25:24.210
We need to support stronger African-American families.

550
00:25:24.420 --> 00:25:28.130
We need to provide the kind of support that people need

551
00:25:28.130 --> 00:25:30.140
in order to be able to do their duty

552
00:25:30.140 --> 00:25:32.410
and fulfill their responsibilities

553
00:25:32.410 --> 00:25:33.970
in raising their children.

554
00:25:33.970 --> 00:25:37.420
But we need also to insist without equivocation

555
00:25:37.420 --> 00:25:41.660
that responsible parenting is the foundation for prosperity

556
00:25:41.660 --> 00:25:44.250
in any society, including in this one.

557
00:25:44.250 --> 00:25:47.310
And that responsible African-American parenting

558
00:25:47.310 --> 00:25:50.400
is an absolute essential prerequisite

559
00:25:50.400 --> 00:25:53.710
for narrowing and ultimately eliminating

560
00:25:53.710 --> 00:25:58.020
the racial inequality gap in America.

561
00:25:58.020 --> 00:25:58.853
Thank you.

